german foreign minister analina babak is  in vilnius as she wraps up a three-day  trip to the three baltic states russia's  invasion of ukraine has dominated the  agenda during her trip speaking  alongside her lithuanian counterpart  bearbox stressed germany's support for  the nato alliance and for strengthening  regional security in the face of the  growing threat from russia she also  played down the prospect of a quick  ceasefire much less an end to the war in  ukraine  and the ukrainians alone get to decide  their future  we should not be under any illusions  about that  as bitter and as difficult as it may be  to accept  having tried time and again to reach a  common security policy in europe with  russia as our partner  putin's russia has forced us to take a  new path  spring in our political correspondent  hans blonde here hans the german foreign  minister there saying europe needs to  protect itself from russia did she offer  any details on how europe should do that  well she's been saying in these baltic  states that she's visited for the last  three days that every inch every last  corner of the nato territory would be  defended against russia in the case of  an attack from russia and the last  corner she was talking about was the  baltic states in those states there has  been for the last five years or so  the so-called tripwire  policy they have stationed smaller units  of nato troops there and if russia  should attack those units and that would  trip the response from the nato  countries  against russia and the baltic states  have said that is not enough that this  is not enough protection for them in the  light of how russia has operated in  ukraine and the german foreign minister  has now said that she supports the  calls by the baltic states to increase  the military pressure the presence of  nato countries nato  troops in their states germany itself  has stationed quite a large number of  troops in lithuania and is prepared to  increase those  now berbach has been touring those three  baltic states over the past few days  all those states have called on germany  to do more for ukraine including  delivering weapons has there been any  movement by the german government on  that front hunts  yes there's been some confusion  especially in terms of communication  from various members of the german  government about this in the last few  days and now i think some clarity has  emerged germany is not going to deliver  heavy weapons out of its own arsenals  because it says it just simply does not  have them available what's going to  happen is that slovenia as the first  country will deliver heavy weapons will  deliver tanks to ukraine and those gaps  that are now that then exist in slovenia  will be filled by supplies from germany  within a few weeks or months so this  kind of chain reaction as it were  has been agreed on in addition germany  is offering to give not only money but  also  maintenance and training for some of the  systems that are going to be supplied  and some of that training is likely to  happen in lithuania and other baltic  states  hans thank you very much our political  correspondent huntsman
 

